1
00:00:00,075 --> 00:00:02,713
Every machine learning engineer knows this loop.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:04,862
Look at the data. Build a feature.

3
00:00:05,275 --> 00:00:09,263
Train. Evaluate. Realize you were wrong, and do it again.

4
00:00:09,675 --> 00:00:12,675
It is the biggest time sink in recommender work, and

5
00:00:12,825 --> 00:00:14,475
almost none of it is creative.

6
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:18,125
So we built an agent that runs the loop by itself.

7
00:00:18,538 --> 00:00:21,775
It reads the problem, reproduces the official baseline,

8
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:26,500
then proposes its own hypotheses, writes the code, trains,

9
00:00:26,512 --> 00:00:28,562
and scores itself on the official metric.

10
00:00:28,962 --> 00:00:32,962
Twenty-three iterations. Zero lines of code written by a

11
00:00:32,975 --> 00:00:33,425
human.

12
00:00:35,093 --> 00:00:36,869
The agent drives four components.

13
00:00:37,231 --> 00:00:40,444
A feature builder that computes every statistic strictly

14
00:00:40,456 --> 00:00:44,030
from the past. A model layer: gradient boosting, a

15
00:00:44,042 --> 00:00:47,091
factorization machine, and item-item models.

16
00:00:47,465 --> 00:00:50,047
The official evaluator, which we never modify.

17
00:00:50,421 --> 00:00:53,586
And a reflection step that reads the scores and picks what

18
00:00:53,586 --> 00:00:54,381
to try next.

19
00:00:55,106 --> 00:00:58,384
Here is a full run. The agent starts from the raw KuaiRand

20
00:00:58,384 --> 00:01:00,920
logs and builds its own feature cache.

21
00:01:01,297 --> 00:01:04,870
One point one million training rows, matching the official

22
00:01:04,882 --> 00:01:09,340
split exactly. Every statistic uses only strictly earlier

23
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:12,158
days. That one discipline was our biggest single gain.

24
00:01:13,085 --> 00:01:16,192
Now it trains, and scores itself with the organizers'

25
00:01:16,204 --> 00:01:18,316
evaluator, which we never modify.

26
00:01:18,692 --> 00:01:22,515
Watch the log. Every iteration records a hypothesis, the

27
00:01:22,515 --> 00:01:25,294
reason behind it, the code change it applied, and the

28
00:01:25,294 --> 00:01:27,090
validation score that came back.

29
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:29,812
This is the moment that matters.

30
00:01:30,225 --> 00:01:33,625
The agent adds collaborative filtering features, and the

31
00:01:33,625 --> 00:01:37,500
score collapses. It inspects feature importance, sees one

32
00:01:37,513 --> 00:01:40,737
feature with four times the gain of anything else, and

33
00:01:40,737 --> 00:01:44,287
diagnoses a leak. The user's own history was scoring the

34
00:01:44,287 --> 00:01:48,800
user. It derives the correction, reruns, and recovers.

35
00:01:49,188 --> 00:01:50,263
No human touched that.

36
00:01:52,093 --> 00:01:55,275
It keeps going, fanning out parallel sweeps across four

37
00:01:55,287 --> 00:01:58,574
workers at a time. It tests a sequence model, a

38
00:01:58,586 --> 00:02:01,803
metric-aware weighting scheme, and a statistics freshness

39
00:02:01,803 --> 00:02:05,657
experiment. All three fail, and it records all three as

40
00:02:05,657 --> 00:02:07,336
failures, because that is what they were.

41
00:02:08,137 --> 00:02:11,600
Finally it declares convergence, under a rule it fixed

42
00:02:11,613 --> 00:02:14,463
before the run started. It names its best validation

43
00:02:14,463 --> 00:02:18,012
checkpoint, and scores the hidden test set exactly once.

44
00:02:20,088 --> 00:02:24,035
The official baseline scores zero point five nine four six

45
00:02:24,243 --> 00:02:27,993
on the hidden test. We reach zero point six zero one five.

46
00:02:28,344 --> 00:02:30,428
On this benchmark the ceiling is not one.

47
00:02:30,779 --> 00:02:33,925
A perfect ranking scores zero point eight six four five,

48
00:02:34,178 --> 00:02:36,590
because a quarter of users have no positive label at all.

49
00:02:36,941 --> 00:02:39,868
Against that real range, we move the baseline's share from

50
00:02:39,868 --> 00:02:42,138
thirty-one percent to thirty-two point four.

51
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:44,079
All on eight CPU cores.

52
00:02:45,095 --> 00:02:47,881
Nothing here is specific to one dataset.

53
00:02:48,286 --> 00:02:51,476
The loop, the leak detection, and the honest logging of

54
00:02:51,476 --> 00:02:54,143
what failed are a template for any ranking problem.

55
00:02:54,524 --> 00:02:56,798
This is what it looks like when iteration is automated,

56
00:02:57,202 --> 00:02:59,095
and the engineer gets to think instead.

